-- title:
--   Theorem 5.5 — constant-answer EPR protocols for RE with soundness $\delta$
-- statement:
--   Let $L\in\mathrm{RE}$ and fix $\delta>0$. There are a constant $a_\delta\in\mathbb N$ and a uniform family of nonlocal games $H_x$, generated by polynomial-time classical verifiers, with polynomial question length and answer length at most $a_\delta$, such that
--
--   1. if $x\in L$, then $\omega^*(H_x)=1$;
--   2. if $x\notin L$, then $\omega^*(H_x)\le\delta$.
--
--   On yes-instances, strategies using finitely many EPR pairs can achieve acceptance probabilities arbitrarily close to one.
--
--   It is the input to the simulation by copies of $\Psi_\lambda$ in Theorem 3.10.
--
--   **Formalization Note** RE is the published `MIPRE.IsRE`. The last sentence is the conclusion that the finite-EPR value of $H_x$ is $1$ on yes-instances.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: Definition 2.15: a uniform family of one-round protocols with a classical randomized verifier that samples question pairs and evaluates decision predicates in time polynomial in the input bit length (prose) → the published `MIPRE.PolyVerifier`, efficient on every input: closed sampler and decider programs, seed length and question length at most $P(|z|)$, decider time polynomial in the total input length, strings longer than $P(|z|)$ rejected; reason: the source defines the class in prose; this is the published machine model of the same polynomial-time two-prover one-round verifier, and it imposes no condition on the language
--   - encoding: answers of length at most $a(|z|)$ → the decider rejects whenever an answer is longer than $a(|z|)$ bits; reason: a strategy can map every overlong answer to a short one without lowering its value, so the value is that of the game with answers of length at most $a(|z|)$
--   - reading: “strategies using finitely many EPR pairs can achieve acceptance probabilities arbitrarily close to one” → the finite-EPR value (supremum over strategies on $m$ EPR pairs, $m\in\mathbb N$) of $H_x$ equals $1$ for $x\in L$; reason: a supremum equal to one is exactly “arbitrarily close to one”
-- source:
--   Dong, Jiang, Qin, Yao, Zhou, A Dichotomy for MIP* in the Presence of Unital Noise, arXiv:2609.39318 (version of 2026-11-03, sha256 56ebc59e…), Theorem 5.5, p.73

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MIPRE_Foundations_ClassMIPStarComputable
import Definitions.Def_UnitalMIP_omegaInf
import Definitions.Def_UnitalMIP_MIPPsi

open scoped Matrix

namespace UnitalMIP

open MIPRE MIPRE.Cost

/-- **Theorem 5.5** (Constant-answer EPR protocols; Dong, Jiang, Qin, Yao, Zhou, *A Dichotomy for MIP* in the Presence of Unital Noise*, arXiv:2609.39318 (version of 2026-11-03, sha256 56ebc59e…), p.73). Let `L ∈ RE` and `δ > 0`. There are a
constant `a_δ ∈ ℕ` and a uniform family of nonlocal games `H_x`, generated by polynomial-time
classical verifiers, with polynomial question length and answer length at most `a_δ`, such that
`x ∈ L ⇒ ω*(H_x) = 1` and `x ∉ L ⇒ ω*(H_x) ≤ δ`. On yes-instances, strategies using finitely many
EPR pairs can achieve acceptance probabilities arbitrarily close to one.

**Formalization Note** The family is `H_z = V.game z` for a published `MIPRE.PolyVerifier` `V`,
efficient on every input (questions of polynomial length `≤ B z`), whose decider rejects every
answer longer than `a_δ`. `ω*` is `omegaStar` (all finite-dimensional states, POVMs). The last
sentence is the conclusion `eprValue (H_z) = 1` on yes-instances (supremum over finitely many EPR
pairs). -/
theorem theorem_5_5 (L : Set BitStr) (hL : MIPRE.IsRE L) (δ : ℝ) (hδ : 0 < δ) :
    ∃ (aδ : ℕ) (V : PolyVerifier), (∀ z, V.Efficient z) ∧
      (∀ z x y a b : BitStr, aδ < a.length ∨ aδ < b.length → ¬ V.Accepts z x y a b) ∧
      ∀ z, (z ∈ L → omegaStar (V.game z) = 1 ∧ eprValue (V.game z) = 1) ∧
        (z ∉ L → omegaStar (V.game z) ≤ δ) := by sorry

end UnitalMIP
